## TODO: mod-source-domain-model

Phase 3 of the multi-source expansion.

### Context

`Mod`, `ModFile`, `ModLink`, `ModDependency`, `Category`, `Paginated`, `SortOption`
in `src/shared/types.ts` are literal modworkshop response shapes, and the Rust
commands (`list_mods`, `get_mod`, ...) return `Result<Value, String>` - raw
provider JSON passed through IPC untyped. Every component consumes modworkshop
quirks directly (`download.url` vs `download_url`, `has_download` with
`download: null`, mod-level vs file-level version, `instructs_template`,
`disable_mod_managers`). Other sources have different shapes AND different
concepts (Nexus file categories main/optional/old; Steam items often have no
version string, only an update timestamp; mod.io has its own dependency model).

### What to do

- Define neutral internal types: ModSummary, ModDetail, DownloadableFile,
  SourceRef, plus per-source extension fields where needed. Rust owns the
  translation: each source module parses its provider JSON into the domain types;
  IPC returns typed structs, never `serde_json::Value`.
- Define a SourceCapabilities descriptor consumed by the renderer: has categories?
  which sort options? batch metadata fetch? direct download vs handoff? auth
  required? BrowsePage renders from capabilities instead of assuming the
  modworkshop feature set. This is what lets NexusBrowsePage fold back into one
  BrowsePage and is the phase where the separate page is deleted.
- The bulk metadata refresh (useModData -> fetchInstalledModsMeta) becomes a
  per-source strategy: modworkshop has the undocumented `ids[]` batch filter
  (~50 ids per request); Nexus has NO batch endpoint and a daily quota on the
  order of a couple thousand requests, so its strategy must be lazy/trickled,
  never "refresh all 300 installed mods on focus".
- Migration path: do it source-by-source behind the same IPC commands rather than
  a big-bang rewrite; modworkshop first, then the nexus module.

---

## TODO: sources-module-restructure

Phase 4 of the multi-source expansion. Largely mechanical after phase 3.

### Context

`api.rs` is a single-host client: modworkshop BASE url, a process-global rate
limiter tuned to modworkshop's undocumented 90 req/min shared budget,
x-ratelimit-remaining semantics, register_download. `nexus.rs` (post-merge) is a
second free-standing client. The proven structural pattern for "several
implementations of one concept" in this codebase is `launchers/` (types.rs trait +
one file per implementation).

### What to do

- Create `src-tauri/src/commands/sources/` mirroring `launchers/`: a ModSource
  trait (list/search, get detail, files, request-download, rate policy, auth
  needs), `modworkshop.rs` and `nexus.rs` as the two implementations. Per-source
  rate limiter instances instead of the global statics (keep the HTTP_CLIENT
  invariant: one shared reqwest client for everything).
- requestPriority.ts foreground/background arbitration currently assumes one
  shared rate-limited pipe; make it per-source.
- Credential storage: settings.json is plaintext. Introduce a secrets story for
  API keys/tokens (candidates: OS keychain via a Tauri plugin, or documented
  plaintext with explicit user consent). Nexus API key moves there; mod.io and
  Steam Web API keys will follow.
- Thumbnails: `thumbnails.rs` hardcodes storage.modworkshop.net and keys the disk
  cache by CDN filename (unique only within that CDN). Key by full-URL hash
  instead; every new source's image CDN origin must be added to BOTH `csp` and
  `devCsp` in tauri.conf.json (a miss fails silently as blank images).

